"Compares alternatives against explicit criteria and agreed importance weights to make evaluation reasoning inspectable.\n\nUse when: Choose among a manageable set of suppliers or proposals. Discuss tradeoffs when no option dominates every criterion.\n\nAvoid when: Noncompensable requirements or unreliable scores make a total score misleading.\n\nVisual grammar: Rows and columns distinguish criteria from candidate options. Cells contain scores on defined, consistently oriented scales. Separate weight and total fields reveal the aggregation method.\n\nBuild: Screen mandatory requirements before scoring eligible alternatives. Define criteria, weights and evidence-based rating anchors together. Score options, calculate totals and examine sensitivity to disputed assumptions.\n\nCheck: Higher scores consistently mean more desirable performance. Weights and score scales are applied as declared. A high total does not override an unmet mandatory condition.\n\nPitfalls: Giving precise numbers to unsupported opinions. Counting the same advantage under several overlapping criteria.\n\nExample: A facilities team compares three cleaning contracts using service coverage, reliability and total cost after enforcing mandatory insurance requirements.\n\nAlternatives: Pugh matrix, Analytic hierarchy process."
